1
00:00:18,350 --> 00:00:25,910
I ended last time by sharing some biographical details about my life.

2
00:00:27,710 --> 00:00:33,770
Some people asked me after, are they really true? So yes, they're absolutely true.

3
00:00:35,710 --> 00:00:42,830
And I told the incidents from two different perspectives.

4
00:00:42,830 --> 00:00:47,830
First as a fault finder and then as a merit finder.

5
00:00:47,830 --> 00:01:07,050
Now, the important thing to keep in mind when we think about the merit finder is that it's not that the merit finder doesn't experience pain or disappointment or anger or humility or fear or disappointment.

6
00:01:08,870 --> 00:01:21,810
You know, it's very disappointing, to say the least, not to be able to pursue one's goal if that goal happens to be a professional career in squash.

7
00:01:22,990 --> 00:01:35,790
It is very humiliating to be the only person who fails out of a program and then to have to walk around for a whole year in William James while feeling like you have a horn coming out of your head.

8
00:01:37,450 --> 00:01:39,130
it's not fun

9
00:01:39,950 --> 00:01:41,090
it's painful

10
00:01:41,770 --> 00:01:50,530
however the difference between a merit finder and sorry, a benefit finder and a fault finder is that

11
00:01:51,230 --> 00:02:03,050
the benefit finder understands that while things don't necessarily happen for the best it is possible to make the best of things that happen things will be okay things will turn out fine

12
00:02:03,050 --> 00:02:10,850
it may take a while until I see the benefit of it. It may take a while until I get over the humiliation or the pain or the disappointment.

13
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:15,280
But that too shall pass.

14
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:24,440
In other words, the benefit finder understands that these feelings are temporary.

15
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:33,460
Gives him or herself the permission to be human and then understands things will turn out fine at the end. I know that. Been there, done that.

16
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:41,160
Part of life, permission to be human, which includes permission to experience these emotions and also the permission to fail.

17
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:52,800
And we'll talk a lot more about this permission to fail when we talk about perfectionism because perfectionism is a real intense fear of failure where we feel that it's all or nothing.

18
00:02:53,560 --> 00:03:02,660
Either we have a complete flawless record or it's useless. It's either phenomenal or a catastrophe, all or nothing.

19
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:14,340
A benefit finder understands that nature, that human nature, dictates that we have painful emotions and human nature, or being human, dictates that we also fail.

20
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:16,300
And that too shall pass.

21
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:24,300
There are many benefits to being a benefit finder.

22
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:32,100
So the first benefit is we simply feel better. We're happier for it. But there are many, many others.

23
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:39,530
For example, Suzanne Thompson from Pomona University did the following research,

24
00:03:39,570 --> 00:03:49,410
where she went to people who had just lost their homes in a fire in California. There were many of them at the time. It was a large wildfire.

25
00:03:49,850 --> 00:03:57,210
And many people lost their homes, and she interviewed them after that. And she distinguished between benefit finders and fault finders.

26
00:03:57,310 --> 00:04:02,370
The benefit finders didn't say, oh, I'm so glad it happened, but they said, well, there's some positive here.

27
00:04:02,370 --> 00:04:13,770
Here, you know, I can start anew, it's a fresh start, I now will appreciate my home more, my family's okay, and I'm relieved, and that's good. So they focused on the positive.

28
00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:24,600
When she followed up with these individuals, those who were benefit finders as opposed to fault finders were happier in the long term,

29
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:32,100
were experiencing more positive moods and less likely to be anxious

30
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:39,300
and had less physical symptoms, both psychological as well as physical consequences.

31
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:43,580
A lot of research on health benefits.

32
00:04:43,580 --> 00:04:54,300
For example, Glenn Affleck did research with individuals who had a heart attack.

33
00:04:55,380 --> 00:05:03,840
And there were some individuals who saw it as a catastrophe, the end of the world. There were others who were certainly not happy about it, but said, okay, so this is a wake-up call.

34
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:11,780
There's actually some positive here because it's telling me I need to take care of myself better, or it helped them revise their personal values.

35
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:18,780
Those individuals who saw the positive, the benefit, who were able to see the wake-up call there

36
00:05:19,420 --> 00:05:23,760
were more likely to survive eight years later, less likely to have a second heart attack.

37
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:27,720
Now, that sounds pretty obvious because, you know, they changed their lifestyle.

38
00:05:28,140 --> 00:05:30,200
But it's not just about changing lifestyle.

39
00:05:31,740 --> 00:05:44,220
Julian Bauer, we talked about last time from UCLA, looked at AIDS patients and identified the benefit finders there.

40
00:05:44,620 --> 00:05:52,920
Not the people who said, oh, I'm so glad I have AIDS, but rather the people who said, as a result of that, I'm able to appreciate certain things more.

41
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:56,980
As a result of that, I focus more on the things that really matter.

42
00:05:57,540 --> 00:06:01,040
As a result of that, I'm getting closer to certain people.

43
00:06:01,940 --> 00:06:07,200
The benefit finders, when she followed up four to nine years later,

44
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:12,580
the benefit finders were more likely to survive, were more likely to be alive.

45
00:06:15,910 --> 00:06:22,980
Research by Laura King and Minor, what they showed was that it can also be used as an intervention.

46
00:06:22,980 --> 00:06:30,180
So it's not just people who were born benefit finders or fault finders, and we know there is a genetic component to it, but there could be an intervention.

47
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:42,480
So they brought in people who had experienced traumas in their lives, and they had them write about these traumas and the, quote, perceived benefit of the trauma.

48
00:06:43,040 --> 00:06:52,820
Those who wrote about the perceived benefit of the trauma were physically healthier and psychologically healthier, happier.

49
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:57,720
Another research on cancer patients.

50
00:06:58,620 --> 00:07:01,360
The women came in and wrote about their cancer.

51
00:07:02,100 --> 00:07:06,620
And they wrote about good things that came out of my experience with cancer.

52
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:12,900
The women who wrote about this, visited the doctor's office less often,

53
00:07:13,120 --> 00:07:24,040
were more likely to survive their cancer just by changing their perspective, by changing their focus. Now, these women did not say it happened for the best.

54
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:34,240
Maybe some did, but most did not. They said, you know, I wish I didn't have this cancer. However, it happened. You know, stuff happens. The question is what we do with it, how we interpret it after.

55
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:38,360
Then they said to themselves, okay, so we got closer to our family.

56
00:07:38,740 --> 00:07:41,080
I'm appreciating life so much more now.

57
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:50,080
I can enjoy the breath or a flower where I couldn't before. My family is so much closer now. I know who my real friends are.

58
00:07:50,820 --> 00:08:00,220
And they found benefit in it, not for the best, but they made the best of the things that happened. And that made the difference even to their mortality rates.

59
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:07,440
And there's a lot of research on optimism, benefit finding, and longevity.

60
