I was living in Singapore at the time.

369
00:47:46,050 --> 00:47:51,510
My best friend was coming over from Indonesia to visit me,

370
00:47:53,370 --> 00:47:56,490
the person who was most important in the world to me at that time.

371
00:47:57,690 --> 00:47:59,190
And her plane crashed.

372
00:47:59,870 --> 00:48:04,430
Silk Air Flight MI-185. Some of you may remember this.

373
00:48:06,330 --> 00:48:08,570
And my world crumbled.

374
00:48:11,720 --> 00:48:23,650
For the first time in my life, I felt that I didn't want to go on anymore. I had no energy. And I called up my family.

375
00:48:24,530 --> 00:48:29,770
And then I called up another person who has made a lot of difference in my life, Nathaniel Brandon.

376
00:48:31,660 --> 00:48:40,500
For various reasons. First of all, Nathaniel Brandon had just, he's a well-known author and psychologist, had just visited Singapore, had just met Bonnie.

377
00:48:43,400 --> 00:49:04,400
And I called him up because he was a psychologist whom I thought could help me, but also because at the age of 45, he lost his wife, who drowned in a pool when she had an epileptic fit, and he found her drowned when he came back home in their pool in their home.

378
00:49:05,180 --> 00:49:16,000
So he had been through that about 25 years earlier. So I wanted to talk to him to get his advice. And I was on the phone and could hardly get my words across.

379
00:49:16,000 --> 00:49:24,400
I was crying constantly. And he said to me, what I'm going to tell you now is not going to make sense, but it's true.

380
00:49:27,660 --> 00:49:29,520
You're going to get over this.

381
00:49:31,340 --> 00:49:33,100
I know you are.

382
00:49:35,580 --> 00:49:37,520
We get over losses.

383
00:49:37,980 --> 00:49:40,020
It's difficult. It's painful.

384
00:49:42,970 --> 00:49:43,510
Cry.

385
00:49:43,970 --> 00:49:45,430
Give the emotions their space.

386
00:49:46,810 --> 00:49:48,390
But you're going to get over it.

387
00:49:48,750 --> 00:49:56,110
We get over painful emotions. We all do. Because if we hadn't, then God help us all.

388
00:49:59,660 --> 00:50:02,760
Because if we hadn't, then God help us all.

389
00:50:04,440 --> 00:50:05,880
And it took a long time.

390
00:50:07,730 --> 00:50:11,470
It was after three months that I could start working again.

391
00:50:12,750 --> 00:50:23,250
It took a whole year for me to feel some of the bounce, some of the passion for life that I felt before.

392
00:50:23,570 --> 00:50:32,530
But now I think about Bonnie, and I smile wistfully, sadly, but I can also appreciate the good times that we had together

393
00:50:32,530 --> 00:50:36,590
and the privilege that I had of meeting her.

394
00:50:38,510 --> 00:50:44,730
We adapt, and that's a good thing. That's an important thing, because God helped us all if we hadn't.

395
00:50:47,710 --> 00:50:50,970
There is another side, though, to that equation.

396
00:50:51,710 --> 00:50:52,770
There is another side.

397
00:50:54,410 --> 00:50:59,230
When we adapt, we also take things for granted.

398
00:50:59,650 --> 00:51:08,910
And that's not a good thing. Because we take for granted our family that we care about so much. And we take for granted our friends.

399
00:51:09,550 --> 00:51:15,670
And we take for granted the food that we're about to have in the dining hall that's served to us. What a luxury.

400
00:51:17,070 --> 00:51:20,190
And we take for granted the person who is sitting next to us.

401
00:51:21,670 --> 00:51:29,130
and we take for granted the classes that we take here because we adapt.

402
00:51:29,930 --> 00:51:40,890
And it's a good thing when it comes to the negatives. It's not such a good thing when we take life for granted. And the question that we need to ask is, is it possible to have it all?

403
00:51:41,050 --> 00:51:47,750
Is it possible to adapt to the negatives, but at the same time not to adapt as much to the positives?

404
00:51:50,010 --> 00:51:57,870
You know, there's a story in the Jewish tradition about a man who was living in one of the shtetls,

