00:54:58,810 --> 00:55:00,430
Now here is the question.

442
00:55:00,810 --> 00:55:07,230
Do things need to get worse before we appreciate what's right in front of us and all around us?

443
00:55:07,770 --> 00:55:10,070
When do we begin to appreciate our health?

444
00:55:10,490 --> 00:55:17,470
When do we begin to appreciate our health? when something goes wrong with us or with someone else? When do we begin to appreciate life?

445
00:55:18,670 --> 00:55:30,250
When ours is in danger or when we lose someone dear to us. And the question that we need to ask ourselves is, must something external, extraordinary, and usually tragic happen

446
00:55:30,250 --> 00:55:38,990
for us to appreciate the ordinary? You see, there are treasures of happiness all around us and within us. right next to us

447
00:55:39,890 --> 00:55:50,670
in the dining hall sitting next to you here in your room at home there are wonderful things things worthy of appreciation all around us and within us but we take them for granted

448
00:55:50,670 --> 00:56:02,190
do we need to wait do we need to wait for that and the answer is no no if we learn gratitude as a way of life if we cultivate the habit

449
00:56:02,190 --> 00:56:03,370
of gratitude

450
00:56:04,550 --> 00:56:14,510
and just as the media has cultivated the fault-finding, we can cultivate benefit-finding. We can cultivate grateful, because when we're grateful for something,

451
00:56:14,730 --> 00:56:17,810
we do not, we no longer take it for granted.

452
00:56:19,270 --> 00:56:30,110
G.K. Chesterton, you say grace before meals, all right, but I say grace before the concert and the opera, and grace before the play and pantomime, and grace before I open a book,

