01:01:18,140 --> 01:01:29,980
You will be reading one of the articles by Robert Emmons and Michael McAuliffe from UC Davis. Let me summarize the two studies there into one.

496
01:01:31,000 --> 01:01:37,580
So what they did was essentially take a random group of people and divide them into four groups.

497
01:01:38,620 --> 01:01:44,460
First group, every night before going to bed, write at least five things for which you're grateful.

498
01:01:45,060 --> 01:01:46,760
Big things or little things.

499
01:01:47,220 --> 01:01:54,420
Second group, write at least five hassles in your life. Five bad things that happened to you.

500
01:01:55,040 --> 01:02:06,500
Third group, write about at least five things that you're better than others at, superior to others. And the fourth group, control group, write about anything that happened to you during the day.

501
01:02:07,640 --> 01:02:19,600
The outcome measures that they looked at was optimism, happiness, physical health, how often they visited the doctor over the six months of the study, one of the studies,

502
01:02:20,940 --> 01:02:25,760
is how generous and benevolent they were toward other people.

503
01:02:26,200 --> 01:02:29,980
And finally, how likely they were to achieve their goals that they set for themselves.

504
01:02:30,800 --> 01:02:32,960
In other words, how successful they were.

505
01:02:33,640 --> 01:02:37,360
The group that performed the worst was the hassles group.

506
01:02:37,760 --> 01:02:43,660
Five bad things that happened to you. The group that performed the best, that was happiest, most optimistic,

507
01:02:43,660 --> 01:03:02,260
Most likely to achieve their goals, most generous and benevolent toward other people, and healthiest was the group that every night before going to bed wrote at least five things for which they were grateful, both psychological and health benefits.

508
01:03:03,060 --> 01:03:12,470
The study was, in many ways, one of the first studies that they conducted. The book just came out recently called Thanks by Robert Emmons,

509
01:03:12,530 --> 01:03:21,650
which is wonderful and traces through a lot more studies and research and exercises that you can do to become more grateful, to have gratitude as a way of life.

510
01:03:22,710 --> 01:03:27,790
Physiological benefits such as our heart variability,

511
01:03:28,390 --> 01:03:34,290
which is a predictor of longevity, which is a predictor of health, is increased.

512
01:03:34,290 --> 01:03:43,210
The parasympathetic system increases when we're grateful, and that induces calm, and that strengthens our immune system.

513
01:03:44,370 --> 01:03:53,450
There are also many benefits to trait gratefulness. It's not just a state of being. It's also about a trait. How do we cultivate that?

