This is also a way to maintain love. People talk about how over time you adapt to your relationships. Well, there's something new to discover about a person every time,

561
01:09:58,310 --> 01:10:03,530
whether it's about my parents, whether it's about my lover, whether it's about my friends.

562
01:10:04,330 --> 01:10:10,870
Creating novel distinctions, looking at it mindfully, focusing on it and maintaining freshness through this focus.

563
01:10:13,410 --> 01:10:14,230
Visualizing it.

564
01:10:15,250 --> 01:10:27,930
And Stephen Koslin from William James Hall, head of our department, did research showing that kids actually visualize most words.

565
01:10:28,150 --> 01:10:38,670
So when you see, when, let's say they say mother, immediately the picture of mother comes up. Whereas very often for us, when we talk, these words are automatic.

566
01:10:38,670 --> 01:10:44,610
We no longer visualize it, which is why it takes kids longer to think because they're still visualizing the words.

567
01:10:44,750 --> 01:10:57,790
It's not automatic anymore, which also explains why kids live like kids, why they're able to appreciate the simplest of things, to be mesmerized and blown away by an airplane up there.

568
01:10:57,970 --> 01:11:08,230
Or when they talk about what they did at daycare, they live like children. And what we have become over the years, we adapted to it and we've become numb to a lot of these things.

569
01:11:08,230 --> 01:11:15,270
So one of the ways to chip away this numbness, one of the ways to do it is to visualize.

570
01:11:16,870 --> 01:11:21,810
And when we visualize, we start to see things once again like kids do.

571
01:11:28,880 --> 01:11:35,540
Next time we'll continue with this, but I want to end today by sharing a story with you.

572
01:11:36,720 --> 01:11:46,070
A story about the person who, to me, more than anyone else, captures what a benefit finder is.

573
01:11:46,070 --> 01:11:53,490
A person who is my role model when it comes to focusing also on the good.

574
01:11:54,830 --> 01:12:00,550
My grandmother was born in 1915 in Siget, Romania.

575
01:12:01,770 --> 01:12:03,930
She had a very regular childhood.

576
01:12:05,450 --> 01:12:11,470
She was the youngest daughter of seven, one more sister, five more brothers.

577
01:12:12,310 --> 01:12:13,990
And they were doing well.

578
01:12:13,990 --> 01:12:36,680
Many of her or her brothers became important rabbis, violinists, musicians, and she was a young girl, happy, living the normal life until 1940, which was when Hitler invaded Romania.

579
01:12:37,520 --> 01:12:42,080
Hitler invaded Romania, and her life changed radically.

580
01:12:42,780 --> 01:12:49,580
Her entire family was taken to one concentration camp after the other, ending up in Auschwitz.

581
01:12:50,980 --> 01:12:59,310
In Auschwitz, she had over a dozen nieces and nephews who were all murdered.

582
01:13:00,490 --> 01:13:07,350
Her five brothers and parents were murdered in front of her very eyes.

583
01:13:07,350 --> 01:13:16,800
And at the end of the war, somehow she and her sister survived.

584
01:13:17,420 --> 01:13:22,950
The British came to Auschwitz to liberate the prisoners.

585
01:13:25,230 --> 01:13:27,210
And when they came, they came in with a doctor.

586
01:13:28,470 --> 01:13:30,790
And the doctor would go through the prisoners.

587
01:13:31,950 --> 01:13:40,470
Most of them were, of course, dead. Those who were alive, he would say, take that person, leave that person here. Because they had limited resources, limited places.

588
01:13:40,690 --> 01:13:44,870
Those prisoners whom they thought would not survive, they left them there.

589
01:13:45,910 --> 01:13:55,450
And he comes to my grandmother, who weighed at the time 26 kilos, which is 54 pounds.

590
01:13:55,810 --> 01:14:05,710
My grandmother was a large woman, 54 pounds, and said, leave.

591
01:14:06,110 --> 01:14:16,790
And then he saw her sister, who was right next to her, who weighed 36 kilos, 79 pounds. And he said, he made this motion to put her on the truck.

592
01:14:18,250 --> 01:14:27,640
And of course, she was too weak to get up. So the British soldiers picked up my grandmother's sister. His name was Shindy.

593
01:14:27,720 --> 01:14:32,220
They picked Shindy up and Shindy held on to her sister.

594
01:14:32,920 --> 01:14:43,640
And she wouldn't let go. And they tried to open her grip. And even though she was weak and sick, they couldn't open her grip.

595
01:14:43,640 --> 01:14:52,840
So the doctor said, fine, take them both. So they took them both, and they put them on the truck. And they were, of course, certain that my grandmother would die.

596
01:14:55,470 --> 01:15:01,330
A month passed, and she survived, though she didn't put on a single gram.

597
01:15:01,930 --> 01:15:06,590
Any day they thought she would die, but she refused to die.

598
01:15:07,150 --> 01:15:10,050
After three months, she started putting on weight.

599
01:15:10,770 --> 01:15:11,810
She survived.

600
01:15:12,770 --> 01:15:19,470
Six months later, she went back to Siget to see what remained from her home.

601
01:15:20,210 --> 01:15:29,290
She arrived there, got off the cart with a horse, and a man by the name of Joseph, Yossi, saw her.

602
01:15:29,950 --> 01:15:36,510
And he saw a woman who was stooped, who was skinned, who still had no hair.

603
01:15:37,190 --> 01:15:48,070
But he recognized those eyes because those eyes were proud. and those eyes were alive and he recognized Goldie and he took her with him

604
01:15:49,190 --> 01:15:57,910
and two weeks later they got married and Goldie got better and got pregnant

605
01:15:58,870 --> 01:16:00,790
and gave birth to a stillborn

606
01:16:02,350 --> 01:16:11,560
and she got pregnant again and once again gave birth to a stillborn they made their way

607
01:16:12,960 --> 01:16:14,420
to Tel Aviv

608
01:16:15,160 --> 01:16:26,100
from Romania and they were caught by the British there was a British mandate at that time they were caught by the British and sent back to a concentration camp in Cyprus

609
01:16:26,940 --> 01:16:35,800
and there in the concentration camp my grandmother in Cyprus had another baby, a baby girl

610
01:16:36,980 --> 01:16:46,340
who survived and then they had another

611
01:16:48,280 --> 01:16:54,820
boy who was stillborn and my grandmother could have no more children her body had just endured too much

612
01:16:59,570 --> 01:17:09,300
My grandmother survived and thrived, as did Shindy. Shindy could have no children, but my mother was her child too, and I was her grandchild as well.

613
01:17:09,740 --> 01:17:20,590
And I remember this was in October of 1988, a few years earlier,

614
01:17:20,830 --> 01:17:32,280
and we went to the cemetery on the day that she passed away to say some prayers over her tombstone, and I went with my grandmother. and we walked there

615
01:17:32,960 --> 01:17:43,080
and my grandmother stood in front of the tombstone and on the tombstone were engraved not just Shindy's name but the name of all the brothers and her parents

616
01:17:44,160 --> 01:17:50,960
and she looked at the tombstone and started to talk to Shindy and said to her we're so fortunate aren't we we're so lucky

617
01:17:51,580 --> 01:17:54,820
aren't we look Talik is here she called me Talik

618
01:17:55,320 --> 01:18:06,520
and then she told her what I was doing and she told her what my brother and sister were doing their grandchildren and about my mother and father and how wonderful things were and she was

619
01:18:06,520 --> 01:18:18,300
talking about the wonderful things and she was crying once in a while missing shiny she was so human just standing there and talking half in Yiddish half in Hebrew once in a while talking to

620
01:18:18,300 --> 01:18:29,600
me and to her sister who saved her life and then she said to me let's go and we walked together

621
01:18:31,140 --> 01:18:40,620
thousands of tombs around us we walked along the path toward the car it was a beautiful October day the sun was out the wind was blowing

622
01:18:40,620 --> 01:18:47,420
the trees were swaying and she suddenly stopped

623
01:18:48,060 --> 01:18:49,200
she stopped

624
01:18:50,600 --> 01:18:57,280
and looked up and she stood so proud so poised and she held my hand so tight

625
01:18:59,240 --> 01:19:09,920
looking up and then looking at me she said Tal, it's such a beautiful world.

626
01:19:11,240 --> 01:19:13,500
Just a pity we have to leave.

627
01:19:14,540 --> 01:19:17,620
And she smiled and we walked.

628
01:19:21,080 --> 01:19:30,780
My grandmother saw thousands of corpses. She saw her family murdered in front of her very eyes. She had three stillborn children.

629
01:19:32,880 --> 01:19:38,120
She did not ignore the terrible things in life. How could she?

630
01:19:39,040 --> 01:19:45,520
However, at the same time, she also refused to ignore the good things in life,

631
01:19:45,960 --> 01:19:51,640
the wonderful things in life. And she was grateful, and she survived.

632
01:19:53,000 --> 01:19:56,420
My grandmother told me that this is a beautiful world.

633
01:19:57,200 --> 01:19:58,480
I believe her.

634
01:19:59,060 --> 01:19:59,840
Thank you.
